    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Laboratory investigations into the physical mechanisms of tracer dispersion and retention in a planar fracture were conducted. Iodide and chloride tracers were flowed in a fracture, first without obstructions and again with a nylon mesh to stimulate transverse mixing and to provide closed pore volumes. Tracer was flowed as a step input across the fracture width. Tracer concentration within the fracture was measured at a series of electrodes embedded in one of the confining plates. The measurements were controlled by and stored in a microcomputer. The theoretical response for linear Taylor dispersion was matched to the data to determine the non-linear parameters velocity and dispersivity. Despite noisy data it was observed that the presence of the nylon mesh did not significantly change the dispersivity. Proposals are presented to improve the quality of the data acquired.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
